WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:04.970
The gorgeous final trailer for Disney's live-action
Beauty And The Beast has arrived!

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:08.450
Yippee ki-yay movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and in this video, I'm going to take a look

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:11.929
at some cool easter eggs and things you might
have missed in it!

00:00:11.929 --> 00:00:15.709
If you spotted anything else, be my guest
and do let me know in the comments below.

00:00:15.709 --> 00:00:19.700
Near the start of the trailer, we see Belle
teaching a young girl to read and pointing

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:21.460
to a page with a Blue Bird on it.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:26.510
The page says "The Blue Bird that flies over
the dark wood" which is a nod to the blue-birds

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:30.810
that fly over the wood at the very start of
the animated movie, and also to the blue-coloured

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:33.590
bird that appears during the song "Something
There".

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:37.699
When we hear that song in the original film,
Belle is trying to bring out the Beast's gentler

00:00:37.699 --> 00:00:40.800
side by encouraging him to feed the birds
in his garden.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.600
At first, they're all scared away with the
exception of a little blue bird who jumps

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:45.860
into his hand.

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:49.659
By the way, if you look closely at what Belle's
wearing as she teaches the young girl in the

00:00:49.659 --> 00:00:54.640
trailer, you'll notice that her cardigan also
has blue birds embroidered into the pattern.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:58.670
And just after this scene, we also see Belle
walking down the village street, reading a

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:02.469
book, just like the original 1991 animation.

00:01:02.469 --> 00:01:07.140
And no one adds humour to this trailer quite
like Gaston when we hear the words "you are

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:11.600
the most gorgeous thing I've ever seen" and
then we see that Gaston is actually talking

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.030
to himself, admiring himself in a mirror.

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:18.979
It's a clever and funny riff on Gaston's opening
song in the original animation where he sings

00:01:18.979 --> 00:01:23.990
"there is only she, who is beautiful as me"
while admiring his reflection in a silver

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:24.990
tray.

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:27.860
Later in the trailer, we also see a few more
shots of Gaston.

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:32.570
In the first, he's standing beneath an inscription
that reads "Gaston The Hunter", a nod to the

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:36.100
portrait of him in the animated movie where
he poses with a gun.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:40.260
However, in this remake, it looks like Gaston's
weapon of choice may have been changed to

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:44.689
a sword rather than a gun, as this picture
of him here shows him posing with a sword

00:01:44.689 --> 00:01:46.719
and the words "Gaston the Victory".

00:01:46.719 --> 00:01:50.969
There's also a slightly different set-up for
Belle's father, Maurice, in this new movie

00:01:50.969 --> 00:01:54.490
when we see him taken prisoner by Beast for
picking a white rose.

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:59.619
In the 1991 movie, he was captured after stumbling
into Beast's castle looking for a place to

00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:00.719
take shelter.

00:02:00.719 --> 00:02:05.659
This use of the white rose in Disney's live-action
movie looks like it was inspired by the 1946

00:02:05.659 --> 00:02:10.130
French film La Belle et la Bête by Jean Cocteau
where Belle's father is caught by the Beast

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:12.590
when he takes a rose from Beast's garden.

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:16.709
And when we see the Beast's horned shadow
appear over Maurice in the grounds, it's a

00:02:16.709 --> 00:02:20.780
callback to the same thing happening to Maurice
as he sits in a chair in the Beast's castle

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:22.430
in the animated feature.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:27.129
In the trailer, it looks like Belle hits Lumiere
over the head with a small wooden stool after

00:02:27.129 --> 00:02:29.629
she's shocked to see a talking candlestick.

00:02:29.629 --> 00:02:33.549
Which reminds me of the scene where Maurice
drops Lumiere after being similarly scared

00:02:33.549 --> 00:02:35.620
in the 1991 film.

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:39.959
This trailer also shows us a bit more of Chip
and we get to see him do his bubbles trick

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:44.989
again just like he did when Belle first took
a cup of tea in Disney's animated movie.

00:02:44.989 --> 00:02:49.420
Just like the original, we see Belle flee
Beast's castle after he flies into a rage

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:53.590
and as she's escaping through the woods, she's
also attacked by a pack of wolves.

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:58.150
After Beast comes to her aid and gets injured,
Belle goes to help him and says "You have

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:00.400
to help me, you have to stand."

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:04.830
This is a subtle nod and acknowledgement of
the fact that the 1991 movie skips over how

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:10.019
Belle managed to lift the unconscious and
enormously heavy Beast on to her horse.

00:03:10.019 --> 00:03:13.939
The second part of the trailer introduces
the new version of the original Oscar and

00:03:13.939 --> 00:03:17.769
Grammy award-winning "Beauty And The Beast"
song, which begins with the line "tale as

00:03:17.769 --> 00:03:21.909
old as time", and is performed by Ariana Grande
and John Legend.

00:03:21.909 --> 00:03:25.720
If you're wondering why it's a duet when it's
just Mrs Potts who sings it in the original

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:29.880
movie, well this is the pop version which
will play over the end credits in the final

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:35.900
film, just like Celine Dion and Peabo Bryson
recorded a pop duet of the song back in 1991.

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:39.870
And as Emma Thompson plays Mrs Potts in the
new movie, we'll (presumably) hear her sing

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:42.790
a more traditional version of the song during
the film.

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:46.810
When Beast shows Belle his library, he mentions
that some of the books are even in Greek.

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:50.989
This is likely a reference to the origins
of the Beauty And The Beast story in the French

00:03:50.989 --> 00:03:55.189
18th-century fairy tale "La Belle et la Bête"
which itself was influenced by the Greek myth

00:03:55.189 --> 00:03:56.519
Cupid And Psyche.

00:03:56.519 --> 00:04:00.579
Later in the trailer, we also see Belle's
hand on a map of the world, a nice hint at

00:04:00.579 --> 00:04:05.439
how she wants 'much more than this provincial
life' as she sings in the 1991 film.

00:04:05.439 --> 00:04:09.689
And it's also a little shout-out to the globe
we see in the Beast's library and in the bookshop

00:04:09.689 --> 00:04:12.319
that Belle loves to visit in that film too

00:04:12.319 --> 00:04:16.410
And there are more nostalgic callbacks to
the original 1991 animation as the "Tale as

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:18.310
old as time" plays over the trailer.

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:22.740
For example, Belle and Beast pick up their
bowls to eat, there are some fantastic shots

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:27.400
of what we'll see during the new Be Our Guest
song, and Gaston and Le Fou sing and dance

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:30.930
in what looks like will be a spectacular scene
in the tavern.

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:35.110
Also while it's a more or less a recreation
of the same scene from Disney's original movie

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:38.980
where Belle sings her song, there's also a
"Hills Are Alive With The Sound of Music"

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:43.720
vibe to the circle shot of Belle standing
on a grassy hilltop overlooking a sweeping

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:44.720
vista.

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:49.139
And there's a very nostalgic moment when Belle
curtseys to Beast just before they dance in

00:04:49.139 --> 00:04:50.139
the ballroom.

00:04:50.139 --> 00:04:54.280
You'll want to look carefully during this
scene, as we get our first glimpse of Cadenza

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:56.580
who's moving gently in the background.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:01.590
Cadenza is a grand piano and a new character
created for the live-action adaptation.

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:05.660
He's played by Stanley Tucci and is described
as a "neurotic maestro".

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:09.790
A grand piano did appear in the ballroom scene
in the first animation, however it wasn't

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:10.820
a live character.

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:14.510
We also get some shots of what looks like
a ball going on in the castle.

00:05:14.510 --> 00:05:18.090
This could be showing us some of Beast's backstory
before he was cursed.

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:21.449
We know that Prince Adam's mother and father,
the King and Queen, have been cast in this

00:05:21.449 --> 00:05:26.040
movie so we could get to see some of what
life was like in the castle before the spell.

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:29.530
So, what did you think of the final trailer
for the new Beauty And The Beast movie?

00:05:29.530 --> 00:05:32.020
And how do you think it's going to compare
to Disney's 1991 animation?

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:35.949
I'd love to hear your thoughts in the comments
below!

00:05:35.949 --> 00:05:39.930
If you enjoyed this video, do please hit that
thumbs up button, share and subscribe.

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:43.919
And you can check out more of my Disney videos
by tapping or clicking right here.

00:05:43.919 --> 00:05:45.759
Thanks for watching and see ya next time.

00:05:45.759 --> 00:05:46.699
Yippee ki-yay movie lovers!

